================================================================================
CATEGORIES - entity model (supersedes EVERY earlier category design)
================================================================================
Three designs preceded this one and all three are dead: (1) flat params
with an inline printed header, (2) a CLI-local active_category with
visible-row indirection, (3) categories synthesized as a VIEW from each
param's full path STRING, with identity = fnv1a64(path) and a per-
container intern table of path strings. Do not resurrect any of them.

What killed (3): the requirement grew to "the user authors and EDITS
categories" (e.g. writes them in a preset file). With a path-derived
identity a rename cannot be non-destructive - the identity IS the thing
being edited, so renaming "Custom/Osc" destroys that node and its whole
subtree and rebuilds it under a new id, losing every hover position
inside it. (3) was the right call while categories were read-only
metadata from CLAP's clap_param_info_t.module; it stopped being right
when they became user data.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
THE ENTITY PATTERN - params/categories are the REFERENCE IMPLEMENTATION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Any future object that needs CX-backed identity, nesting, or reparenting
should follow this shape rather than re-deriving one. The rules:
1. The owning module keeps a FLAT, tombstoned store. A survivor's
   storage never moves (CROSS-CUTTING INVARIANT at the top of this file).
   Nesting is NOT expressed by nesting the storage.
2. Every entity gets a uid minted BY THE OWNING MODULE from a file-
   static, never-reset counter, so it is unique program-wide and not
   merely within one container. 0 is reserved to mean "none" / "mint me
   one". (params.c already does this for params; categories get their
   own separate counter - different namespaces, so sharing one buys no
   uniqueness and only makes both sequences gappy and hard to read.)
3. The module hands out an OPAQUE handle - a pointer to the entity's own
   struct, which carries self_container + self_local_id - plus a resolve
   function that turns it back into (container, local_id). Nothing above
   the module ever sees the struct. See param_get_handle/param_handle_
   resolve; mirror that shape exactly, don't invent a second convention.
4. Parent/child structure is a `parent_uid` FIELD on the entity. That is
   what makes reparenting a field write instead of a storage move, and
   it is the whole reason this model can do (STEP F) what the path-
   string model could not.
5. app_data wraps each entity KIND in exactly one DataOps table whose
   functions take only the handle - so the table is owner-agnostic and
   every owner shares it (cx_param_ops is already like this).
6. ContextId = MAKE_ID(one namespace per entity KIND, uid). One
   namespace per KIND, never per owner, because rule 2 makes uids
   program-unique.
7. Structural-change notification = a generation counter on the store.
   app_data polls it and emits DATA_EVENT_CHILDREN_CHANGED on the owning
   node; app_intrf diffs the whole subtree (STEP A). The data layer never
   has to describe WHAT changed, only that something under here did.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
TARGET-LIST CONTRACT (verified - settled, do not re-litigate)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
ui_layer's only HARD obligation is that no stored ContextId ever names a
DESTROYED context. Beyond that a view may store whatever it likes in a
target list; a stale-but-still-valid id is the view's own business and
self-heals. Verified in smp_groovebox_ui_cli.c: helper_nav_context_
single_purpose_set scans the parent's LIVE children for the stored id
and falls back to index 0 when it isn't found, so the stored id is a
hint and the live child list is the source of truth. What makes this
safe is DataOps.id's "program-unique, never reused for a different
object", which the minting counters of rule 2 guarantee. The
UiStalePolicy modes are therefore ERGONOMICS (re-anchor to a sibling
instead of snapping to index 0), not safety.

