STEP A - app_intrf: whole-subtree diff resync   [DONE 2026-09-20]
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
IMPLEMENTED as mark/sweep/add rather than by building two id->parent sets:
the CX tree is ALREADY indexed by id in app_intrf->cx_hashtable, so "does
this desired node exist?" is an O(1) ht_get and only the other direction
needs help. CX gains a uint64_t sync_stamp and APP_INTRF a counter
incremented once per resync (a fresh CX is calloc'd to 0 and stamps start
at 1, so a never-marked CX can't look current by accident). Three static
helpers, then app_intrf_cx_resync is six lines:
  app_intrf_cx_mark_desired  - recurse the DATA subtree, stamp every CX
      that already represents one of its nodes. Takes the parent's
      ContextId and stamps ONLY when the CX already sits under that same
      parent, so a node that moved is left unstamped on purpose - the
      sweep frees it and the add phase re-creates it in its new place.
      That remove+add is what a move degrades to until CX_MOVED exists.
  app_intrf_cx_sweep         - recurse the CX subtree backwards (cx_
      subtree_free shifts the array down), free anything unstamped, and
      only descend into survivors.
  app_intrf_cx_add_missing   - recurse the DATA subtree top down, create
      what has no CX. A created node's subtree is materialised by
      app_intrf_cx_children_create so it is not descended into; only an
      already-existing node is. Guards the one case the sweep cannot
      reach - an id living under a DIFFERENT parent outside this subtree
      - by leaving it alone rather than ht_set-ing over it and stranding
      the old CX. Nothing moves today; CX_MOVED is the real fix.
