ORIGINAL SPEC, for reference:
WHAT: replace app_intrf_cx_resync's one-level, interleaved remove/add
with a diff computed BEFORE anything is mutated:
  1. walk the data layer under parent_cx  -> desired {id: (parent, idx)}
  2. walk the CX tree under parent_cx     -> current {id: (parent, idx)}
  3. REMOVED = current - desired, ADDED = desired - current
  4. apply ALL removals, THEN all additions top-down
WHY FIRST: every later step needs a tree deeper than one level, and this
is the only step that can be verified with NO new features - its
observable behaviour must be identical to today's.
WHY THE ORDER: the same ContextId can leave one parent and arrive under
another in a single reconciliation (a param whose category changed).
Interleaved per-level apply can ht_set the new CX and then ht_remove the
id, leaving a live CX that is no longer in the hashtable - silent, and it
surfaces much later as an inexplicable navigation failure. Removals
strictly first makes that safe. (STEP F inverts this order once CX_MOVED
exists - see there, and note the inversion is deliberate.)
NOT A BUG TODAY: the tree under a plugin is currently flat, so one level
genuinely suffices. This is prerequisite work, not a fix.
