read from a stored string. If the answer is yes, DATA_CAP_PROPERTIES
comes back with it.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STEP F - DEFERRED until user-authored categories actually exist
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Do NOT build this speculatively. Build it when a preset file (or any
other user-facing editor) can actually write categories, and only if
losing hover on rename turns out to matter in practice.
- Operation_SetCategory in param_set_value, mirroring Operation_Change
  Name: a ui-only field, no rt side, no ring-buffer message. Must bump
  the STEP B generation counter.
- A category RENAME is NOT structural under this model: mutate name,
  emit DATA_EVENT_CHANGED. No CX churn, no lost hover. This is the main
  thing the entity model buys over the dead path-derived design, where a
  rename changed the id and destroyed the whole subtree.
- A MOVE (param to another category, or a category to another parent) is
  one parent_uid/category_uid field write. To make it non-destructive:
  * CX_MOVED added to CxEventType. Detection is nearly free once STEP
    A's diff exists: "in both sets, different parent".
  * CxEvent needs NO new field. A move carries the OLD parent/index -
    exactly what a view needs to re-anchor, same as CX_REMOVED.
  * app_intrf reparent = pop from the old parent's cx_children array,
    push to the new one, update cx_parent/idx. The HASHTABLE ENTRY DOES
    NOT CHANGE - it is keyed by data_id, which is precisely what did not
    change. app_intrf_cx_children_pop already does the array surgery; it
    just needs splitting from its free/ht_remove half.
  * APPLY ORDER INVERTS to ADD (top-down) -> MOVE -> REMOVE: additions
    first so a move's destination exists even when it is brand new,
    removals last so survivors have been moved out of doomed subtrees
    before those are freed. This is the opposite of STEP A's order and
    getting it backwards silently frees live nodes.
  * ui_layer cost is ~3 lines: on CX_MOVED mark the view touched so
    ui_layer_state_reconcile returns UI_RECONCILE_CHANGED and it
    redraws. NO pruning is needed - see the TARGET-LIST CONTRACT above;
    the context still exists, nothing dangles, and a view that filed the
    id under the old parent re-derives on its own. Applying an item
    stale policy on a move is OPTIONAL polish (re-anchor to a sibling
    instead of snapping to index 0).

